1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,008
How does the bank know its records match the card network's?

2
00:00:04,058 --> 00:00:09,384
In Lesson 1, ecom checked every payment against the processing centre's own record.

3
00:00:09,434 --> 00:00:13,704
In Lesson 34, ecom counted its own side of each day.

4
00:00:13,754 --> 00:00:17,736
This lesson puts the two sides next to each other, line by line.

5
00:00:17,786 --> 00:00:19,872
Picture two quiet mistakes.

6
00:00:19,922 --> 00:00:23,832
One payment timed out on our side, but the processing centre booked it.

7
00:00:23,882 --> 00:00:27,264
Another we marked paid, but the centre reversed it overnight.

8
00:00:27,314 --> 00:00:29,232
Both look fine in our own records.

9
00:00:29,282 --> 00:00:34,488
At settlement, the bank is short, and nobody knows which payment caused it.

10
00:00:34,538 --> 00:00:36,792
Think about checking your bank statement at home.

11
00:00:36,842 --> 00:00:38,664
You put it next to your own notes.

12
00:00:38,714 --> 00:00:40,632
You tick each line that matches.

13
00:00:40,682 --> 00:00:43,080
And you circle every line that does not.

14
00:00:43,130 --> 00:00:46,968
Every day, for each card network, the bank does the same.

15
00:00:47,018 --> 00:00:52,776
This is reconciliation: proving that two sets of books agree, line by line.

16
00:00:52,826 --> 00:00:59,928
Our side is every money movement ecom recorded: each charge, each reversal, each refund.

17
00:00:59,978 --> 00:01:03,528
Their side is the processing centre's own report for that day.

18
00:01:03,578 --> 00:01:08,832
ecom fetches that report through the card zone, and it arrives with masked card numbers only.

19
00:01:08,882 --> 00:01:13,224
ecom does the matching; the card zone only hands over the report.

20
00:01:13,274 --> 00:01:17,544
Lines are paired by kind, by payment, and by the network's reference number.

21
00:01:17,594 --> 00:01:24,336
Each pair must then agree on four things: whether money moved, the amount, the terminal, and the day.

22
00:01:24,386 --> 00:01:26,208
The day's totals are checked too.

23
00:01:26,258 --> 00:01:28,080
Any difference is a break.

24
00:01:28,130 --> 00:01:34,272
A break is one line where the two books disagree, or a line that one side has and the other does not.

25
00:01:34,322 --> 00:01:38,376
A payment still unknown when the day closes is a break as well.

26
00:01:38,426 --> 00:01:43,704
Every break becomes exactly one exception: a case with an owner and a deadline.

27
00:01:43,754 --> 00:01:45,576
Then a person works it.

28
00:01:45,626 --> 00:01:50,688
If they find proof that the two sides really agree, they resolve it with that evidence.

29
00:01:50,738 --> 00:01:55,344
If the difference is real and cannot be recovered, they ask to write it off.

30
00:01:55,394 --> 00:01:59,784
A write-off means the bank accepts the difference and closes the case.

31
00:01:59,834 --> 00:02:04,296
Because a write-off is money the bank gives up, someone else must decide it.

32
00:02:04,346 --> 00:02:07,248
Every change to an exception is written down.

33
00:02:07,298 --> 00:02:09,120
Let's watch the product.

34
00:02:09,170 --> 00:02:13,104
A bank admin opens Reconciliation in the bank's portal.

35
00:02:13,154 --> 00:02:17,088
They choose a card network and a business day, and press Reconcile.

36
00:02:17,138 --> 00:02:22,224
A run like this happens every night on its own; this button runs a day again.

37
00:02:22,274 --> 00:02:24,096
The run finishes.

38
00:02:24,146 --> 00:02:25,968
Totals shows Differ.

39
00:02:26,018 --> 00:02:32,712
The items list shows every line with its match state: matched, mismatched, or missing on one side.

40
00:02:32,762 --> 00:02:36,144
A mismatched line gives its reason: amount differs.

41
00:02:36,194 --> 00:02:39,288
Our amount and the network's amount sit side by side.

42
00:02:39,338 --> 00:02:41,496
The admin opens Exceptions.

43
00:02:41,546 --> 00:02:45,672
Every unmatched item is here, with its owner and when it is due.

44
00:02:45,722 --> 00:02:47,544
One is marked Overdue.

45
00:02:47,594 --> 00:02:52,152
The admin presses Take, and the page says: you own this exception now.

46
00:02:52,202 --> 00:02:54,024
They add a note about what they checked.

47
00:02:54,074 --> 00:02:58,824
The difference is real, so they write the reason and choose Ask to write off.

48
00:02:58,874 --> 00:03:01,512
The page says a second admin decides.

49
00:03:01,562 --> 00:03:05,832
When the same admin looks at the exception again, there is no approve button for them.

50
00:03:05,882 --> 00:03:12,120
Instead, a line says they asked to write this off, and another admin approves or rejects.

51
00:03:12,170 --> 00:03:17,064
A second admin logs in, opens the same exception, and reads the reason.

52
00:03:17,114 --> 00:03:18,936
They approve the write-off.

53
00:03:18,986 --> 00:03:20,856
The state becomes Written off.

54
00:03:20,906 --> 00:03:27,360
The audit trail at the bottom shows every step: who opened it, who took it, who asked, and who approved.

55
00:03:27,410 --> 00:03:29,232
What does this protect?

56
00:03:29,282 --> 00:03:35,304
Without reconciliation, two books can disagree for weeks, and the bank learns about it from a buyer's complaint.

57
00:03:35,354 --> 00:03:38,256
With it, every difference is found the next day.

58
00:03:38,306 --> 00:03:41,112
Every difference has one owner and a deadline.

59
00:03:41,162 --> 00:03:45,216
And no difference disappears because one person decided to ignore it.

60
00:03:45,266 --> 00:03:50,952
It protects the bank's money, and it protects the merchant from a payment lost between two systems.

61
00:03:51,002 --> 00:03:52,824
Back to the statement.

62
00:03:52,874 --> 00:03:58,368
Every line ticked, every difference circled, and every circle in someone's hands.

63
00:03:58,418 --> 00:04:01,416
Next: limits on what one website may take.
